What this product takes from that material:

- engine/resources/dqc_0015_non_negative_elements.txt is derived from the
  DQC_US_0015 element lists: the union of the element local names in the
  twelve lists above (6,696 names, no values, no other columns). Its header
  comment names the source lists.
- engine/engine.py and engine/xbrl.py re-implement the logic of the three
  rules in Python from the XULE source and the rule documentation: rule
  scopes, the eleven axes DQC_US_0117 checks, the DQC_US_0015 member and axis
  exclusions, and the tolerance functions. They are this product's own code.
- No XULE, CSV or documentation file of the DQC is included, no DQC software
  is executed, and no per-filing DQC result was used (the repository's tests/
  folder, which holds rule output for named filings, was not downloaded).

The DQC rule files also point to https://xbrl.us/dqc-patent for a patent
notice. On 2026-10-03 that address redirected to plugin/xule/PatentNotice.md
in the same repository, a file its master branch does not contain.


2. FinReason Cup Task 3 starter kit (MIT), not included
-------------------------------------------------------

Source:  https://github.com/The-FinAI/IEEE-bigdata-cup, folder
         finreason_task3/. The validator, judge and data-preparation code
         this product runs or imports is the same from commit
         089a2a3a8156b3532819dcf9a8f6a2f368b7bb93 (2026-09-24) through
         11f8673e3645c4a00fb47f668b71fa1cf270159f (2026-09-29), the last
         change to the folder as of 2026-10-03.
Licence: MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 FinReason Cup 2026 Task 3 organizers.

This product does not include, vendor or modify the kit. reproduce.py runs the
kit's scripts/validate_submission.py from the user's copy as a subprocess, and
tools/score_split.py and tools/holdout_overlap.py --score import the kit's
evaluation and task3_core packages (the deterministic judge). The kit is found
at ./kit, or through --kit or the FINREASON_TASK3_KIT environment variable.


3. FinReason Cup Task 3 data (CC BY 4.0), not included
------------------------------------------------------

Ranked inputs: Hugging Face dataset YanAdjeNole/FinReason-Task3, revision
f32033b1dc0c1b0ef080740ed5ee02ef6b04c931 (2026-09-28), files
development_inputs.jsonl and test_inputs.jsonl, published by the FinReason
Cup organizers under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

Practice set: Hugging Face dataset TheFinAI/FinMR, revision
3b1babd0487c1d5a7229f7a26a1654d3fb693e92 (332 cases), Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International. FinMR is one of the three tasks of the
FinAuditing benchmark, and the organizers' kit asks users of the data to cite
Y. Wang et al., "FinAuditing: A Financial Taxonomy-Structured Multi-Document
Benchmark for Evaluating LLMs", in Proceedings of the 49th International ACM
SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval
(SIGIR '26), pp. 3456-3463, 2026, https://doi.org/10.1145/3805712.3808578
(BibTeX in README.md, Citation). The kit's scripts/prepare_public_dev.py
turns it into data/public_dev.jsonl and data/public_dev_inputs.jsonl.

This product contains no case text and no answer key. split.json holds
practice case ids (DEV_000000 to DEV_000331) and counts computed from the
practice inputs. reproduce.py holds the sha256 digests of the published input
files and of this product's own prediction files.
